-- Serbia deep-dive content (English). A mature, centralized-clearance
-- e-invoicing regime (SEF), live and mandatory B2B since 1 Jan 2023,
-- now layering in a separate e-delivery-note (e-otpremnica) system for
-- goods movement, rolling out in two phases (1 Jan 2026 / 1 Oct 2027).
-- Live-researched against Serbia's own Legal Information System
-- (pravno-informacioni-sistem.rs), the Ministry of Finance's legal-text
-- pages (mfin.gov.rs), the official SEF portal (efaktura.gov.rs), and
-- cross-checked against Paragraf.rs (a reputable Serbian legal
-- publisher, flagged as a secondary compiler rather than the gazette
-- itself where relied on for penalty figures) plus several independent
-- vendor/advisory summaries (Comarch, VATupdate, Fonoa, Fiscal
-- Solutions, docloop.rs, injac.rs, edicomgroup.com, Sovos, Cleartax)
-- that corroborate the two-phase e-otpremnica structure. Every URL
-- cited as a milestone source_url or deep-dive portal was independently
-- fetched in this session -- see PROGRESS.md's dated build entry for
-- the full citation trail, including flagged gaps: the exact new date
-- for a separately-reported "expanded B2B reform" package (sourced only
-- via a secondary advisory citing an unlocated primary MoF
-- announcement), and whether B2C/fiscal-receipt invoicing sits inside
-- or outside the e-Faktura mandate (not independently confirmed).

  'Mature centralized-clearance regime: mandatory B2B/B2G e-invoicing live since 2023 via the SEF platform, now layering in a separate e-delivery-note (e-otpremnica) system for goods movement, rolling out in two phases through 2026-2027',
  'This deep-dive page reflects the Zakon o elektronskom fakturisanju and the Zakon o elektronskim otpremnicama as published in Serbia''s Official Gazette (Sl. glasnik RS), and publicly available government guidance as of early August 2026, and is provided for general awareness, not legal or tax advice. Confirm current requirements directly with Serbia''s Ministry of Finance, SEF support channels, or a Serbian tax/legal adviser before relying on this page for compliance decisions.',
  'Serbia''s e-invoicing rollout began with the public sector: suppliers to public-sector buyers were required to issue e-invoices via SEF from 1 May 2022, with public-sector entities required to issue to companies from 1 July 2022. The general B2B mandate followed on 1 January 2023, making e-invoicing (issuance and receipt) compulsory across the entire private sector for VAT-registered taxpayers. A separate law, the Zakon o elektronskim otpremnicama, now adds a parallel e-delivery-note system, rolling out in two phases: public sector, excise-goods traders, and carriers from 1 January 2026, extending to general private-sector B2B goods movement from 1 October 2027.',
  'A SEF-compliant e-invoice is XML based on UBL 2.1, with a Serbia-specific CIUS aligned to the EU''s EN 16931 semantic standard. Transmission is a centralized clearance (CTC) model -- every invoice is submitted to and validated by SEF (Sistem elektronskih faktura) before reaching the recipient, functionally similar to Italy''s SDI, with SEF also acting as the invoice repository. The e-otpremnica system uses the same UBL 2.1-based approach, managed by a "Central Information Intermediary," accessible via the national eID portal, web UI, mobile app, or API.',
  'The e-invoicing mandate applies to all VAT-registered taxpayers and all entities using the public procurement system, with no company-size or revenue threshold identified in the law''s text -- foreign companies operating via a local tax ID or fiscal representative are included. The e-otpremnica mandate is instead phased by category of activity: Phase 1 covers the public sector, excise-goods traders, and carriers; Phase 2 (1 October 2027) extends to general private-sector B2B goods movement. Whether B2C/fiscal-receipt invoicing sits inside or outside the core e-Faktura mandate was not independently confirmed in this research.',
  'Most businesses trading with Serbia already have a live SEF e-invoicing obligation -- the mandate has been in force since 2023. The newer work is the e-otpremnica (e-delivery note) system: businesses in the Phase 1 categories (public sector, excise goods, carriers) needed to be ready by 1 January 2026 with inspection enforcement now fully live, while other businesses moving goods B2B should plan ahead of the 1 October 2027 Phase 2 date.',
  'Serbia backs both statutes with real, tiered fines. The e-invoicing law (Arts. 18-21) and the e-delivery-note law (Arts. 13-14) both set penalty ranges by offender category (legal entities, entrepreneurs, responsible individuals), sourced here via Paragraf.rs''s consolidated legal text -- a reputable secondary compiler, not the raw gazette itself, so article-by-article attribution should be treated as indicative pending direct verification of the gazette text.',
  'Serbia has required B2G e-invoicing since May-July 2022 and mandatory B2B e-invoicing (issuance and receipt, via the centralized SEF platform) since 1 January 2023, for all VAT-registered taxpayers. A separate, newer law adds e-delivery notes (e-otpremnica): mandatory from 1 January 2026 for the public sector, excise-goods traders, and carriers, extending to general private-sector B2B goods movement from 1 October 2027. A further "expanded B2B" e-invoicing reform package (new fields, stricter validation, a new penalty regime) has been reported by secondary sources as postponed to "end of 2026," but its exact date could not be confirmed against a primary government announcement.',
  '🇷🇸'
FROM countries WHERE code = 'RS';






